{
  "an6.20:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 6.20 ",
  "an6.20:0.2": "2. Warm-hearted ",
  "an6.20:0.3": "Mindfulness of Death (2nd) ",
  "an6.20:1.1": "At one time the Buddha was staying at Ñātika in the brick house. ",
  "an6.20:1.2": "There the Buddha addressed the mendicants: ",
  "an6.20:1.3": "“Mendicants, when mindfulness of death is developed and cultivated it’s very fruitful and beneficial. It has freedom from death as its objective and culmination. ",
  "an6.20:1.4": "And how is mindfulness of death developed and cultivated to be very fruitful and beneficial, with freedom from death as its objective and culmination? ",
  "an6.20:2.1": "As day passes by and night draws close, a mendicant reflects: ",
  "an6.20:2.2": "‘I might die of many causes. ",
  "an6.20:2.3": "A snake might bite me, or a scorpion or centipede might sting me. ",
  "an6.20:2.4": "And if I died from that it would be an obstacle to my progress. ",
  "an6.20:2.5": "Or I might stumble off a cliff, or get food poisoning, or suffer a disturbance of bile, phlegm, or piercing winds. ",
  "an6.20:2.6": "And if I died from that it would be an obstacle to my progress.’ ",
  "an6.20:2.7": "That mendicant should reflect: ",
  "an6.20:2.8": "‘Are there any bad, unskillful qualities that I haven’t given up, which might be an obstacle to my progress if I die tonight?’ ",
  "an6.20:3.1": "Suppose that, upon checking, a mendicant knows that ",
  "an6.20:3.2": "there are such bad, unskillful qualities. Then in order to give them up they shou